{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"git show 75d707a86e299a0cb0396bb719ea2002121bd66a:project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py | Select-String -Pattern '_kitmanifest|manifest' | Select-Object -First 12; git ls-tree -r --name-only 75d707a86e299a0cb0396bb719ea2002121bd66a project-trajectory | Select-String 'manifest'; git show 75d707a86e299a0cb0396bb719ea2002121bd66a:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\\\\[.*SN-029\\\\]' -Context 0,25; git show 75d707a86e299a0cb0396bb719ea2002121bd66a:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -Skip 1660 -First 16; rg -n 'block format|## <|reviewer|fresh' project-trajectory/prompts -g '*review*' -g '*critique*' \"","aggregated_output":"\r\n        bootstrap_manifest as _kitmanifest,\r\n        bootstrap_manifest as _kitmanifest,\r\nMAPPING = _kitmanifest.MAPPING\r\nmapping_entries = _kitmanifest.mapping_entries\r\nproject-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/bootstrap_manifest.py\r\n> [need.SN-029]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\n  tags = [\"unattended\", \"loop\"]\r\n  need =\"\"\"**An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can.** Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human \r\njudgement only when the declared approval level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its \r\nown, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is \r\npermitted to attest. On every tier released to automation, an approval still happens as a review from a declared \r\nperspective against the row's actual evidence, and leaves a record naming who or what approved it — releasing a tier \r\nmeans the review is delegated, never skipped.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"\"\"The unattended layer only pays for itself if a walk-away run actually walks away — so what stops it must be \r\nexactly the set of judgements the owner reserved, and nothing else. A three-value gate-authority enum (`attended` / \r\n`single-approve` / `autonomous`) cannot express \"TCs are human-held but LLRs are not\", the distinction an owner \r\nactually wants to dial, and leaves each consuming table free to re-interpret the same word with its own fail-safe \r\ndirection. Answering \"has this been approved?\" by walking git for the newest commit where a row reads `Verified` is \r\nsound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit — precisely the sanctioned path an amendment \r\ndetector ignores — so the blessed way to amend an attested requirement is invisible to every consumer, and a \r\nstakeholder need has no anchor at all (an SN has no Status cell).\"\"\"\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"A cumulative level names the highest tier a human still approves, compared against a separately \r\nderived SPINE STAGE (which tier is in process) via a declared, auditable mapping. Every failure direction — an \r\nunreadable stage, an out-of-range level, a wrong-typed dial — resolves toward MORE human involvement, because the \r\nfailure that matters is a machine approving something a human meant to hold. Each acceptance is anchored ON THE \r\nACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW — the commit whose tree carries the accepted text, plus a digest of that row's normative \r\ncells — never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact. Text that has moved away from what was accepted \r\nsurfaces regardless of any Status movement, the one signal the sanctioned amend-and-flip path would otherwise hide, so \r\nan AMENDED requirement drops the derived stage exactly as a newly introduced one does. Each automated approval on a \r\nreleased tier appends a durable record (row, transition, acting reviewer, commit) distinguishable from a human \r\napproval by query.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-033]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A stakeholder can read each stakeholder need and recognize the \r\noutcome they asked for without knowing how the repository is built.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"\"\"A need written in internal engineering vocabulary can remain technically current while becoming unreadable \r\nto the person whose intent it is supposed to preserve. The resulting failure is an approved requirement spine that no \r\nstakeholder can independently validate.\"\"\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"The rule applies specifically to each SN `need` cell, not to engineering requirements or acceptance \r\nevidence. A declared check reports the row and phrase when a need cell contains an internal path, implementation-only \r\nidentifier or process citation; a reviewed exception list distinguishes names that are themselves user-facing \r\ninterfaces. The DevStg-Reqs review evidence includes the resulting findings.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-034]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-04\"]\r\n  tags = [\"launcher\", \"templates\"]\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A person setting up to work on the repository can start each \r\nof the two universal contributor actions — preparing their development environment, and resuming the automated \r\ndevelopment loop — with a single action from the repository's front door, on Windows, macOS and Linux alike (one click \r\nwhere the platform allows it; one obvious command where it does not).\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"\"\"Setup friction is where a new contributor or fresh machine silently diverges from the declared toolchain, \r\nand a resume path that needs remembered commands is a resume path that rots. A single visible entry point per platform \r\nmakes the sanctioned path the easy path. On Linux desktops a bare double-click is unpromisable (the platform requires \r\nmarking a file executable first), so the promise there is one action, not one click.\"\"\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"Both entry points exist at the repository root for each supported platform in a fresh scaffold and \r\nin this repository; each launches its action as a single step on that platform, or documents the one platform-required \r\nstep where a single click is impossible; the adopter-facing guide names the capability and invites reuse. This row is \r\nthe ONE home for launcher-class obligations — root visibility, one action per platform, both universal contributor \r\nactions. Spanning those three is what holds it at need tier: the demotion test asks whether a need is a single \r\nrequirement in a need's clothing, and this row is not one requirement, so the test does not reach it.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n[interface.IF-129]\r\nowner = \"scripts/acceptance_record\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/baseline_snapshot\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"split_changed_cells: the approved and traced changed cells, before and after\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-131]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The rules read the live source AST instead of parsing the retired docs/architecture.md MODULE MAP block back. One walk, one grammar: the module keys and import names are exactly what the map rendered, so every downstream join (stem resolution, CMP membership) is unchanged.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/gen_arch_map\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/check_trajectory\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"scan_inventory, contracts_grammar_findings, file_contracts, file_grammar_findings, owner_files, ContractsGrammarError\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:8:     REDACTED BY CONSTRUCTION: the reviewer gets the diff plus the requirement\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:31:     scratch area, and that the launcher, not the reviewer, records the\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:44:     set is classified by the reviewer, a fail-closed one a coverage-extension\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:53:     stays the ordinary scope, so an ordinary or loop reviewer keeps the\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:60:     phrase fails them, and the fake-CLI harness discriminates a reviewer\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:64:You are an INDEPENDENT reviewer launched by the unattended coordinator ({scripts}/agent_loop.py) or by an attended launcher — a fresh context that did NOT write this code. Assume the implementer was careful but missed something, and hunt for it. The work item(s) under review are:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:67:When a change alters one side of a requirement/interface relationship, the same scoped review record must name the changed side and its counterpart and record the independent reviewer's semantic decision: carry the corresponding change or justify retaining the counterpart. Reference-existence checks support this reading but do not discharge it. For a decomposition, record each retained child's independent decision or verification purpose and why further splitting would add no independent value; do not substitute a row count for that judgment.\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:68:Their specs are the Done-when you map coverage against; nothing here narrows the reading scope below, which stays the diff itself. Review ONLY (1) the diff of the work under review — the exact reading scope is `git diff {trunk}...{head} -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'` (three dots against the CURRENT trunk, so a station refresh never re-feeds you trunk's own merged work; session telemetry, verdict records and generated artifacts are excluded because they are records of the process, not the work) — and (2) the requirement surface it must satisfy: AGENTS.md, {process_doc}, the docs/requirements registries, and each work item's own spec file under docs/work/ (plus its `specref` target where that points elsewhere) — the spec of record. Grep the registry rows the diff cites; never read a whole registry file or docs/log.md end to end. If this diff adds or changes requirement rows (SN/SR/TC under docs/requirements), also sweep them against the EXISTING registries — the new rows AND the historical rows they touch — for any contradiction, overlap, or attribute/limit conflict, and raise each as a finding (mark it 'for clarity' at MINOR when it is a wording ambiguity sharper SN/SR/TC language would resolve, not a defect). If the diff under review is a DevStg-Reqs/DevStg-Tests approval (a Status-change commit closing a `[phase]-[g*]` gate), the batch-scoped approval hierarchy is a REQUIRED input: generate it with `{scripts}/trace.py --approve <phase>` and confirm the approved SN->SR->LLR/TC batch — its Requirement/AC, LLR Detail, TC Method/Expected, and any cited rubric — is coherent and complete before endorsing the gate. Flag status.md prose that contradicts a declared policy file's current value as a finding. Do NOT read or trust the implementer's own session notes or self-assessment — a leaked self-assessment collapses review finding-rates several-fold. Run the harness yourself and quote real output; believe nothing you did not observe — but run each instrument ONCE and quote its SUMMARY (`python {scripts}/check.py --jobs 0`: the Check summary block; `python {scripts}/trace.py --strict-integrity`: the final line), never the full advisory stream, which spends hundreds of WARN lines of your context on no finding. Drive the diff's REAL shipped code paths — construct the scenario and run the actual function or flow it changes; primitive probes and plausibility reading are supporting evidence, never the verdict's basis. Before hunting, name the worst failure classes THIS change admits (silent wrong content, fail-open, data loss) and hunt those first, severity-ordered. Hunt within the scope bound {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\" sets. Tie severity to reachability: a BLOCKER or MAJOR states a scenario reachable in normal use, and a fail-open, data-loss or security path counts only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it; a rare or contrived path is MINOR at most, or dismissed under {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\". An input outside the spec's declared set is yours to classify, not the set author's: one the change fails closed on is a coverage-extension note, written as prose outside the finding lines with no severity tag, so it never moves the count; one the change fails open on stays a finding at its severity. An APPROVE must mean you tried to break it and failed: map each spec Done-when item to its covering test or call it UNCOVERED, and where the diff adds a regression test for a fixed defect, confirm that test fails on the pre-fix behavior. For each test the diff adds or changes, ask: does it drive the interface the requirement names, not handler source? would it fail on a plausible wrong implementation? is each Done-when item covered? Of each new file and function, ask whether it repeats, exactly or in intent, something that already exists ({process_doc} §3, \"Consolidate, don't duplicate\"), and raise each repeat as a finding. This is an INDEPENDENT parallel review — do not debate another reviewer. Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <file:line> -> issue -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. When that concrete change ADDS a check, guard, warn, or invariant, the same line must also carry one clause naming why the defect cannot be made UNREPRESENTABLE instead — a stricter type, a deleted code path, or a single owning boundary that validates once and is trusted thereafter (the `antidote` skill's question, \"the smallest change that makes this fix unnecessary\", which you cite, not restate). Warn-first: this binds the remedy's WORDING, not the verdict — no finding is refused, downgraded, or blocked for want of the clause. It is NOT owed by a MINOR `for clarity` wording finding, nor by validation at a genuine trust boundary (where one lies: {process_doc} §3 \"When a guard is owed\"); only by a guard that compensates for a REACHABLE bad state the design could have made unreachable. Naming why construction is unavailable is enough — specifying the structural fix is not your job when it exceeds the diff's scope. Then exactly one machine line:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:8:     REDACTED BY CONSTRUCTION like the reviewer brief: the critic gets the\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:11:     DO NOT RE-WRAP. Same reason as the reviewer template.\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:14:You are an INDEPENDENT critic. In coordinator use, you are launched by the unattended coordinator (scripts/agent_loop.py) as a fresh context that did NOT produce this artifact, wearing a DIFFERENT hat from the implementer; in attended use, you are a human-chosen fresh reviewer who did not produce it. Your job is subjective-quality judgment: say WHERE and WHY the artifact is or is not good enough, judged ONLY against the WRITTEN RUBRIC below — never a fresh opinion of your own, and never a lax test case. Do NOT read or trust the implementer's session notes, docs/status.md, docs/log.md, or any self-assessment (a leaked self-assessment collapses a critic's finding rate). Produce the artifact yourself from the recipe below (agent CLIs read local images/renders natively; if your model cannot, judge the text/description proxy and SAY SO), inspect it, and score it against the rubric's numbered anchors.\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:24:In coordinator use, Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format;\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:26:In attended use, identify the human-chosen reviewer and sitting/review record\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:29:record at the assigned path. In either case, identify the fresh non-author\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
