l/B3 judge duplicated scope. These independently cover the requested plan judgment; no additional B# anchor is needed. This does not adjudicate every clause of SN-029 beyond this item's scope. Transfer caveat: applying the protocol's objective-verifier evidence to plan quality remains an extrapolation, not a proven instrument.\n\nAnchors judged: DevStg-Reqs, DevStg-Tests, DevStg-Impl, G4, B1, B2, B3 and B4. The overlap around P4/P8 is explicitly tests versus implementation; P5/P6/P7 divide the identity carrier, producer and consumer; P1/P12 divide authorship and the independent sitting; P10/P13 divide entry authorship and final anchoring. These are substantive seams rather than duplicated deliveries. The sequential order is buildable apart from the missing P3 scaffold delivery below. P8 is large but has an explicit atomicity reason in the spec. No separate padding, seam-duplication or dependency finding is warranted.\n\nDELTA judgment: within is supported. The plan identifies the new binding state and grammar, their producer and consumers, the changed lane and marker inputs, and the new module boundary. P5–P8 carry those behavioral changes; P1/P12 carry the normative reconciliation, including SR-208's deliberate change in scope. The exception, parent intersection, trunk dial, narrowed marker arm, retirements and old-binding migration have concrete deliveries. The new module's distribution boundary is the omission below.\n\n- [MAJOR] DevStg-Reqs -> docs/plans/WI-851/plan.md P3, with P10's new-file migration list: P3 moves the held-status implementation into a new scripts/held_status.py and changes shipped callers to import it, but no row delivers its scaffold mapping. At the Reviewed commit, project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/bootstrap_manifest.py enumerates shipped scripts explicitly; it maps acceptance_record.py and text_then_act.py and has no wildcard that would pick up held_status.py. Following P3 as written therefore leaves fresh scaffolds without a required impo